WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.393 --> 00:00:02.890
(clicking and rattling)

00:00:02.890 --> 00:00:06.160
- Here it is. Here's the original one.

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:08.560
I think there's this misnomer
that my orange coat is dead,

00:00:08.560 --> 00:00:10.090
but no, it's just dying quickly.

00:00:10.090 --> 00:00:12.040
There's just a good
amount of this going on.

00:00:12.040 --> 00:00:13.972
But I think this thing has a
couple more years, for sure.

00:00:13.972 --> 00:00:16.760
(dramatic music)

00:00:16.760 --> 00:00:19.010
So this is my desk.

00:00:19.010 --> 00:00:23.910
I sit here all day and I
work and I look at this map.

00:00:23.910 --> 00:00:27.340
Whenever I'm daydreaming,
I just look at this map.

00:00:27.340 --> 00:00:29.410
(dramatic music)

00:00:29.410 --> 00:00:30.808
I bought this map off eBay

00:00:30.808 --> 00:00:32.560
and it was in like an elementary school,

00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:34.210
and now it's in my office.

00:00:34.210 --> 00:00:36.980
I notice something every time I look at it

00:00:36.980 --> 00:00:38.530
that kind of irks me.

00:00:38.530 --> 00:00:43.530
That something is this, this zero.

00:00:45.540 --> 00:00:47.920
This zero, which is not just one number,

00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:51.003
but it's actually a corresponding
line that looks like this.

00:00:53.716 --> 00:00:55.966
(rumbling)

00:00:59.500 --> 00:01:01.090
Let's back up here for a second.

00:01:01.090 --> 00:01:03.728
The earth is divided into lines.

00:01:03.728 --> 00:01:06.311
(upbeat music)

00:01:09.330 --> 00:01:11.990
Sometimes they're curved
like they are on my map,

00:01:11.990 --> 00:01:13.250
and whether or not
they're curved or straight

00:01:13.250 --> 00:01:15.330
has to do with this concept of projection,

00:01:15.330 --> 00:01:16.670
which I'm not going to talk about here.

00:01:16.670 --> 00:01:20.610
I've talked about plenty
using a knife and a globe

00:01:20.610 --> 00:01:22.540
back in an old Vox video that I made.

00:01:22.540 --> 00:01:25.030
But just know that the earth
is divided up into lines,

00:01:25.030 --> 00:01:26.640
latitude and longitude lines.

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:29.820
Ones that go up and down and
ones that go from side to side.

00:01:29.820 --> 00:01:31.680
All of these lines in the grid

00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:33.620
have a number associated with them.

00:01:33.620 --> 00:01:36.050
So for the up and down
lines, there's a zero point.

00:01:36.050 --> 00:01:39.500
And then anywhere to the
right of that or east of that

00:01:39.500 --> 00:01:43.480
starts to go up from zero to 180,

00:01:43.480 --> 00:01:46.730
with a little E next to it
for east of the zero line,

00:01:46.730 --> 00:01:49.500
and then everything to
the left or the west of it

00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:54.500
has the same thing, zero
to 180 with a W for west.

00:01:54.820 --> 00:01:57.500
Okay, that center point is the zero point

00:01:57.500 --> 00:02:02.110
from which all east and
west things are measured.

00:02:02.110 --> 00:02:04.020
The same thing for these
side to side lines.

00:02:04.020 --> 00:02:05.670
If you go up from here,

00:02:05.670 --> 00:02:07.450
you're gonna start to
get higher and higher

00:02:07.450 --> 00:02:09.450
until you get to 180 degrees north.

00:02:09.450 --> 00:02:12.750
Or if you go south, you
will go 180 degrees south,

00:02:12.750 --> 00:02:13.993
which is the south pole.

00:02:15.180 --> 00:02:17.030
Finding out where the zero point should be

00:02:17.030 --> 00:02:19.850
for these horizontal lines
is actually quite easy.

00:02:19.850 --> 00:02:23.100
The earth has these two natural poles,

00:02:23.100 --> 00:02:27.030
which make for a natural
top and bottom of the earth,

00:02:27.030 --> 00:02:29.840
and halfway in between those two poles,

00:02:29.840 --> 00:02:33.300
you have this perfect
center point, the equator,

00:02:33.300 --> 00:02:36.240
which is a very natural
place to put this zero point

00:02:36.240 --> 00:02:37.840
and to measure everything from.

00:02:37.840 --> 00:02:41.070
The up and down lines
are totally arbitrary,

00:02:41.070 --> 00:02:44.100
where you decide them, like,
who's the say that, like,

00:02:44.100 --> 00:02:47.020
it couldn't be right here in
Russia, and this could be zero,

00:02:47.020 --> 00:02:50.490
and everything could go
west and east from here,

00:02:50.490 --> 00:02:53.270
or Japan for that matter?

00:02:53.270 --> 00:02:56.823
Well, in fact, a long time
ago, that's how it used to be.

00:02:58.460 --> 00:03:01.640
These maps with these grids
were really, really important

00:03:01.640 --> 00:03:02.700
for navigating the world.

00:03:02.700 --> 00:03:05.520
And so everyone had to
choose a zero point.

00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:08.410
And most would just make their capital

00:03:08.410 --> 00:03:10.700
or their home base the zero point.

00:03:10.700 --> 00:03:11.880
If you lived in Japan,

00:03:11.880 --> 00:03:14.570
you would just make Japan the zero point

00:03:14.570 --> 00:03:17.700
and the rest of the
coordinates would be measured

00:03:17.700 --> 00:03:20.260
against that one zero
point, which was Japan.

00:03:20.260 --> 00:03:23.393
And same if you lived in
France or Washington, D.C.

00:03:24.820 --> 00:03:28.340
This was fine because back
then countries kind of just

00:03:28.340 --> 00:03:30.480
fought wars and competed with each other.

00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:33.160
They weren't really
collaborating or aligning

00:03:33.160 --> 00:03:35.630
or trading as much as they do today.

00:03:35.630 --> 00:03:37.670
And so everyone could
just have their own maps

00:03:37.670 --> 00:03:41.310
centered around their place
and they did just fine.

00:03:41.310 --> 00:03:45.480
But then around the 1800s, a
few things started to happen

00:03:45.480 --> 00:03:48.156
that completely changed everything.

00:03:48.156 --> 00:03:51.650
(dramatic music)

00:03:51.650 --> 00:03:54.040
First, countries and empires,

00:03:54.040 --> 00:03:56.050
instead of just competing
and fighting with each other

00:03:56.050 --> 00:03:57.870
started to actually say like,

00:03:57.870 --> 00:04:01.210
we should align with each
other and trade and be friends

00:04:01.210 --> 00:04:03.390
because that's better for everyone.

00:04:03.390 --> 00:04:06.980
Pair this with the fact that
map making and sea navigation

00:04:06.980 --> 00:04:09.640
was becoming like a big industry.

00:04:09.640 --> 00:04:12.810
It was no longer just
the king ordering maps

00:04:12.810 --> 00:04:15.350
to be made for their like expeditions.

00:04:15.350 --> 00:04:16.910
But now it was like private businesses

00:04:16.910 --> 00:04:21.069
going out and doing things
that private businesses do.

00:04:21.069 --> 00:04:22.430
(screeching)

00:04:22.430 --> 00:04:26.980
And so there was now like a
demand for good seafaring maps.

00:04:26.980 --> 00:04:29.600
In short, map making was trending.

00:04:29.600 --> 00:04:32.100
Seems like a pretty good time to be alive.

00:04:32.100 --> 00:04:33.250
Okay, and there was one more thing

00:04:33.250 --> 00:04:34.710
that was happening around this time

00:04:34.710 --> 00:04:37.235
that was super important, which is, this:

00:04:37.235 --> 00:04:40.010
(upbeat music)

00:04:40.010 --> 00:04:42.000
Trains, trains everywhere.

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:44.670
Trains will just like
popping up everywhere.

00:04:44.670 --> 00:04:47.240
And trains were also trending.

00:04:47.240 --> 00:04:50.420
People were now able to travel
like across the country,

00:04:50.420 --> 00:04:51.660
not just like on their horses,

00:04:51.660 --> 00:04:54.330
but on this whole system of trains

00:04:54.330 --> 00:04:56.410
where you could buy a
ticket, get on a train,

00:04:56.410 --> 00:04:59.110
and end up somewhere
hundreds of miles away.

00:04:59.110 --> 00:05:00.820
And you know, what trains run on,

00:05:00.820 --> 00:05:04.430
besides coal, is time,
timetables, schedules.

00:05:04.430 --> 00:05:05.980
How do you know when your
train is going to arrive

00:05:05.980 --> 00:05:07.870
and when it's gonna leave and
when it's gonna get there?

00:05:07.870 --> 00:05:10.940
Time, some universal set time.

00:05:10.940 --> 00:05:14.000
The problem is, back in the 1800s,

00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:16.210
there was no universal set time.

00:05:16.210 --> 00:05:17.810
Check out this piece of paper.

00:05:17.810 --> 00:05:20.220
Today, all of these
cities and towns would be

00:05:20.220 --> 00:05:23.840
just on Eastern Standard
Time, one time zone.

00:05:23.840 --> 00:05:26.530
But back then, every
town had their own time,

00:05:26.530 --> 00:05:28.760
depending on when it was noon for them.

00:05:28.760 --> 00:05:30.930
If they were a little bit east
or a little bit west of here,

00:05:30.930 --> 00:05:33.380
that would be different
than in Washington, D.C.

00:05:33.380 --> 00:05:35.763
Total nightmare for trains.

00:05:36.890 --> 00:05:40.130
The lifeblood of trains is time.

00:05:40.130 --> 00:05:43.530
And this time mish-mash
just wasn't gonna work.

00:05:43.530 --> 00:05:47.490
Okay, so what does all of
this have to do with this?

00:05:47.490 --> 00:05:49.053
Look at this guy.

00:05:49.053 --> 00:05:50.690
(dramatic music)

00:05:50.690 --> 00:05:52.210
You probably don't know
who this person is.

00:05:52.210 --> 00:05:53.770
I didn't know who this person was.

00:05:53.770 --> 00:05:57.030
He was the US president
that no one remembers.

00:05:57.030 --> 00:06:00.200
This US president in the late 1800s

00:06:00.200 --> 00:06:01.680
saw a problem with all of this.

00:06:01.680 --> 00:06:04.360
He realized that having
a bunch of different maps

00:06:04.360 --> 00:06:06.090
with a bunch of different zero points

00:06:06.090 --> 00:06:09.420
based on whoever was
making it was a problem.

00:06:09.420 --> 00:06:13.010
And he realized that not
having a universal time zone

00:06:13.010 --> 00:06:14.680
or some sort of universal way

00:06:14.680 --> 00:06:17.990
of making the same time for
everyone was also a problem.

00:06:17.990 --> 00:06:22.990
So he invited 41 countries to
come here to Washington D.C.,

00:06:23.200 --> 00:06:26.130
where I live, and to sit around and think

00:06:26.130 --> 00:06:29.240
and talk about where this
zero point should be.

00:06:29.240 --> 00:06:31.730
Where should the center of the world be

00:06:31.730 --> 00:06:36.180
so that we can measure
time as well as navigation.

00:06:36.180 --> 00:06:39.090
All maps could be
normalized to this one spot.

00:06:39.090 --> 00:06:42.060
So after a moment of
a bunch of white dudes

00:06:42.060 --> 00:06:44.520
sitting around in a room
in Washington, D.C.,

00:06:44.520 --> 00:06:47.360
which is kind of what's happening
today in Washington, D.C.,

00:06:47.360 --> 00:06:48.520
they came to a vote.

00:06:48.520 --> 00:06:51.930
And that vote almost unanimously said

00:06:51.930 --> 00:06:55.469
that the zero line should be right here.

00:06:55.469 --> 00:06:58.219
(dramatic music)

00:07:00.910 --> 00:07:02.600
This line, if you zoom in,

00:07:02.600 --> 00:07:04.710
goes right through London, England.

00:07:04.710 --> 00:07:06.590
It actually goes through
a little outskirts town

00:07:06.590 --> 00:07:07.570
called Greenwich.

00:07:07.570 --> 00:07:09.820
Greenwich is the home
of the Royal Observatory

00:07:09.820 --> 00:07:11.060
of the British Empire.

00:07:11.060 --> 00:07:14.280
This would be the place
where the Prime Meridian,

00:07:14.280 --> 00:07:17.400
or the zero point for the entire world,

00:07:17.400 --> 00:07:18.960
east and west, would start.

00:07:18.960 --> 00:07:21.130
Every country at the conference agreed

00:07:21.130 --> 00:07:22.190
that this should be the case,

00:07:22.190 --> 00:07:24.210
except for the Dominican Republic,

00:07:24.210 --> 00:07:26.150
at that time called Santo Domingo.

00:07:26.150 --> 00:07:28.350
They said, no, they were the one country

00:07:28.350 --> 00:07:29.700
that didn't like this proposal.

00:07:29.700 --> 00:07:33.860
And France and Brazil actually
abstained diplomatically,

00:07:33.860 --> 00:07:35.027
which was kind of them saying like,

00:07:35.027 --> 00:07:36.460
"Oh, we don't agree with this."

00:07:36.460 --> 00:07:38.800
France continued to make their maps

00:07:38.800 --> 00:07:40.780
with Paris as the center point,

00:07:40.780 --> 00:07:42.590
but eventually they got on board as well.

00:07:42.590 --> 00:07:44.430
So that gets to the
crux of my big question

00:07:44.430 --> 00:07:46.700
of why the zero right there,

00:07:46.700 --> 00:07:48.610
which is a bunch of people voted on it.

00:07:48.610 --> 00:07:52.050
But why were they so down with
Britain having the zero point

00:07:52.050 --> 00:07:53.780
being the center of the world?

00:07:53.780 --> 00:07:56.280
Well, the answer is because it was 1884

00:07:56.280 --> 00:08:00.110
and Britain was just
really good at the ocean.

00:08:00.110 --> 00:08:02.810
The British Empire was by far the biggest,

00:08:02.810 --> 00:08:05.290
most powerful navy that
had the most experience

00:08:05.290 --> 00:08:07.300
with maps and ocean-faring.

00:08:07.300 --> 00:08:09.910
And so it was kind of a
natural choice to let Britain,

00:08:09.910 --> 00:08:13.540
the king of the ocean,
have this center point.

00:08:13.540 --> 00:08:18.270
And from then on, maps started
to be drafted with Greenwich,

00:08:18.270 --> 00:08:20.730
this Royal Observatory,
as the center point

00:08:20.730 --> 00:08:22.810
for the east/west divide in the world.

00:08:22.810 --> 00:08:26.250
Over time, this exact point,
exactly where it's drawn,

00:08:26.250 --> 00:08:29.850
has shifted a tiny bit
because of better tools

00:08:29.850 --> 00:08:30.683
to measure things.

00:08:30.683 --> 00:08:33.080
And so it's actually like not literally

00:08:33.080 --> 00:08:34.880
on the Royal Observatory anymore,

00:08:34.880 --> 00:08:36.390
but it's effectively in the same place

00:08:36.390 --> 00:08:38.500
that was drawn in this 1884 conference.

00:08:38.500 --> 00:08:42.100
But they also established that
all time in the entire world

00:08:42.100 --> 00:08:45.550
would be measured against
this same spot, Greenwich.

00:08:45.550 --> 00:08:48.680
Noon at Greenwich, at
the Royal Observatory,

00:08:48.680 --> 00:08:52.750
would be the peg against
which all other time was set.

00:08:52.750 --> 00:08:54.860
They called this Greenwich Mean Time.

00:08:54.860 --> 00:08:57.274
So you end up getting a
map that looks like this.

00:08:57.274 --> 00:09:00.024
(dramatic music)

00:09:03.140 --> 00:09:05.820
Zoom in and you see
Greenwich is right here.

00:09:05.820 --> 00:09:08.980
That's GMT, Greenwich Mean Time, okay?

00:09:08.980 --> 00:09:12.640
You go west of here and you
start to get GMT minus one

00:09:12.640 --> 00:09:14.330
or minus two, minus three.

00:09:14.330 --> 00:09:15.340
You can come all the way back

00:09:15.340 --> 00:09:16.580
to the east coast of the United States.

00:09:16.580 --> 00:09:18.640
And this time zone is Greenwich meantime,

00:09:18.640 --> 00:09:21.730
minus five right now,
sometimes it's minus four,

00:09:21.730 --> 00:09:22.820
if there's daylight savings,

00:09:22.820 --> 00:09:25.040
and daylight savings is dumb and whatever,

00:09:25.040 --> 00:09:26.480
but you get the idea.

00:09:26.480 --> 00:09:29.780
The more you go west, you go
back from Greenwich Mean Time.

00:09:29.780 --> 00:09:31.310
If you go east, it's the opposite.

00:09:31.310 --> 00:09:32.870
You go forward of Greenwich Mean Time.

00:09:32.870 --> 00:09:36.360
So if it's noon at Greenwich,
then this next time zone,

00:09:36.360 --> 00:09:40.740
it would be 1:00 pm, 2:00 pm,
3:00 pm, 4:00 pm, et cetera.

00:09:40.740 --> 00:09:42.730
So these time zones have been marked up

00:09:42.730 --> 00:09:45.330
in these kind of strange shapes.

00:09:45.330 --> 00:09:48.490
Eventually, if you go 12
steps away from Greenwich,

00:09:48.490 --> 00:09:52.520
12 time zones, you'll end
up here, at this mess.

00:09:52.520 --> 00:09:56.023
This mess is the International Date Line.

00:09:56.970 --> 00:09:59.200
This is where Greenwich Mean Time,

00:09:59.200 --> 00:10:02.250
plus 12 or minus 12 ends up at this line,

00:10:02.250 --> 00:10:03.910
which is where a new day starts.

00:10:03.910 --> 00:10:06.010
So let me give you an example
of what this would look like.

00:10:06.010 --> 00:10:09.130
Imagine it's 4:00 pm
in Greenwich, England,

00:10:09.130 --> 00:10:11.480
right here at this Royal Observatory.

00:10:11.480 --> 00:10:16.010
It is 4:00 pm GMT on May 28th.

00:10:16.010 --> 00:10:17.710
If anyone wants to send
me a birthday gift,

00:10:17.710 --> 00:10:20.170
May 28th, May 28th.

00:10:20.170 --> 00:10:24.660
It's May 28th at 4:00 pm
GMT, Greenwich, Mean Time.

00:10:24.660 --> 00:10:28.050
Sitting, chilling in
the Royal Observatory,

00:10:28.050 --> 00:10:29.070
which I've never actually been to.

00:10:29.070 --> 00:10:31.100
I need to go to the Royal Observatory.

00:10:31.100 --> 00:10:32.326
I need to go to the Royal Observatory.

00:10:32.326 --> 00:10:34.840
I'm going to do that this
year, next year, at some point.

00:10:34.840 --> 00:10:37.063
Okay. So it's 4:00 pm, May 28th.

00:10:38.240 --> 00:10:41.910
Across the ocean here in
Washington, D.C., at that time,

00:10:41.910 --> 00:10:46.910
you can see that we go from
4:00 pm minus five, so 11:00 am.

00:10:48.190 --> 00:10:50.260
Okay, so it's 11:00 am
here in Washington, D.C.

00:10:50.260 --> 00:10:52.570
at that exact time on May 28th.

00:10:52.570 --> 00:10:54.290
Head across the United States

00:10:54.290 --> 00:10:55.890
to the west coast of the United States,

00:10:55.890 --> 00:10:58.310
to Seattle or Portland, and right there,

00:10:58.310 --> 00:10:59.927
it's three hours earlier than it is here.

00:10:59.927 --> 00:11:03.620
And you're now at 8:00
am. So it's 8:00 am here.

00:11:03.620 --> 00:11:06.080
It's still 4:00 pm in Greenwich.

00:11:06.080 --> 00:11:07.940
It's still 4:00 pm in London.

00:11:07.940 --> 00:11:09.440
Now you head into the Pacific Ocean.

00:11:09.440 --> 00:11:14.440
Keep going west until you
get to French Polynesia,

00:11:14.640 --> 00:11:17.730
at which time it's five in the morning.

00:11:17.730 --> 00:11:21.370
You're now at GMT minus 11.
It's five in the morning.

00:11:21.370 --> 00:11:25.070
And then you get right
up to this messy line.

00:11:25.070 --> 00:11:28.210
If you were to be in the Baker Islands,

00:11:28.210 --> 00:11:32.890
which is like a US, like
random uninhabited island

00:11:32.890 --> 00:11:37.210
in the middle of the Pacific
Ocean, you would be at 4:00 am.

00:11:37.210 --> 00:11:39.420
So it's 4:00 pm over in Greenwich.

00:11:39.420 --> 00:11:43.010
It's 4:00 am on this random
island in the Pacific.

00:11:43.010 --> 00:11:46.100
So then cross this messy looking line.

00:11:46.100 --> 00:11:50.360
It's still 4:00 am, but
now it's the next day.

00:11:50.360 --> 00:11:52.510
It's May 29th at 4:00 am.

00:11:52.510 --> 00:11:56.700
Here in Fiji at the same
time, 4:00 pm at Greenwich,

00:11:56.700 --> 00:12:00.800
it's now 4:00 am the next day, May 29th.

00:12:00.800 --> 00:12:03.990
So we went from being 12
hours behind Greenwich

00:12:03.990 --> 00:12:07.580
to being 12 hours in front of Greenwich,

00:12:07.580 --> 00:12:10.870
just by going over this
International Date Line.

00:12:10.870 --> 00:12:13.110
You could do this exact same
thing going the other way.

00:12:13.110 --> 00:12:15.930
Start heading east, and
you see that, you know,

00:12:15.930 --> 00:12:18.330
it's 5:00 pm and 6:00 pm and 7:00 pm.

00:12:18.330 --> 00:12:20.820
Head all the way over into Asia,

00:12:20.820 --> 00:12:23.570
and now you're getting closer to seeing

00:12:23.570 --> 00:12:27.610
how that 4:00 am happens
when you get over to Fiji.

00:12:27.610 --> 00:12:29.190
Now, the caveat to all
of this is, of course,

00:12:29.190 --> 00:12:31.520
is that some of these
countries switch by an hour

00:12:31.520 --> 00:12:33.860
for like daylight savings
type things and whatever,

00:12:33.860 --> 00:12:34.693
but ignore that.

00:12:34.693 --> 00:12:36.460
Imagine there wasn't daylight savings.

00:12:36.460 --> 00:12:38.290
Right now, in this moment,
where daylight savings is,

00:12:38.290 --> 00:12:39.830
at least in the United States,

00:12:39.830 --> 00:12:43.100
this holds across the whole world.

00:12:43.100 --> 00:12:47.563
Someday I'll make a video
about this whole messy debacle.

00:12:48.440 --> 00:12:50.350
But the point of all of this is to say

00:12:50.350 --> 00:12:53.500
that because of this conference in 1884

00:12:53.500 --> 00:12:58.500
to reconcile train timetables
and to make maps more uniform,

00:12:59.170 --> 00:13:02.860
we now have this
navigation and time system

00:13:02.860 --> 00:13:04.270
that rules our whole world.

00:13:04.270 --> 00:13:07.690
And Britain is just sitting at
the center of it, of course.

00:13:07.690 --> 00:13:09.240
The British Empire may have fallen

00:13:09.240 --> 00:13:10.580
and the world has moved on

00:13:10.580 --> 00:13:13.340
from Britain being the center
of the world in a lot of ways,

00:13:13.340 --> 00:13:16.870
